75 #edb/ref 17592186430022 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430036 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430028 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430036 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430036 1077 "selection" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430036 1078 #edb/ref 17592186430022 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430036 1079 #uuid "c6c197bd-bdee-548f-8f17-1f8666979616" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430018 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430020 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430023 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430024 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430025 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1077 "selection" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1078 #edb/ref 17592186430025 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430037 1079 #uuid "cd353f17-4f1a-5904-b62b-8c4277a7e856" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430010 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430014 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430027 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430029 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430030 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1077 "selection" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1078 #edb/ref 17592186430030 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430038 1079 #uuid "fab2c55e-07fe-5e9d-9f01-1f1219cae67d" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430034 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430039 1069 "q-126523" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430039 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430039 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x-{x}^{4}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430039 1072 #uuid "37b63c9c-6c92-5f44-bb20-4d23e2c51ff4" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430039 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-{x}^{4})=1-4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{1+{(1-4{x}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{2-8{x}^{3}+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430033 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430040 1069 "q-109146" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430040 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{y}$ that lies between $y=1$ and $y=4.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430040 1072 #uuid "394bc722-72f5-5959-b491-d15c78d36cfd" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430040 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=1$ to ${y}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y\\sqrt{\\frac{4y+1}{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{y(4y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430037 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430041 1069 "q-109186" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430041 1071 "![](images/a7/a70a12201e99ded8eed85b797c760862814e1f0a50129020fa6d3b35d4334394.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts (see figure above). What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430041 1072 #uuid "5eff5095-a0c0-5448-9e3d-e45ec11b1792" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430041 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =\\pm n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430038 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430042 1069 "q-126540" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430042 1071 "![](images/68/68a30adbe260a15c8514ceec2291da4be77af2335f8999d512ffd46c7b18c6bc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=2x-{x}^{2}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430042 1072 #uuid "6d75b243-8939-5146-b57c-2c5e64292e74" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430042 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-{x}^{2})=2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2-2x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430036 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430043 1069 "q-126580" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430043 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430043 1072 #uuid "b3f52ad3-220f-5e23-b87e-dca64195a02f" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430043 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi x\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430035 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430044 1069 "q-109269" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430044 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430044 1072 #uuid "e501ffe3-f914-5273-b8d8-73d2e5bc3adb" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true] [17592186430044 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})=\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}(3-x)}{3}(\\frac{x+1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-x)(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3+2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {[3x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534630 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-076d4f91-52ee-5265-9716-346105e7c17c" 17592186430003 "answer-0cba0bba-3874-5f31-9506-90e69f99ee58" 17592186430004 "answer-32cf01df-92ff-5fdb-a4e6-5c90197c9574" 17592186430005 "answer-358a047c-5548-544e-bf1d-f9959f75de54" 17592186430006 "answer-3a00d32e-d7d5-5b7b-ae72-6a9a030e3ace" 17592186430007 "answer-3f9acf2a-3897-5212-ae15-bafc3627e866" 17592186430008 "answer-422bd46c-effb-5d0b-904d-3ee35544d973" 17592186430009 "answer-51c26f7f-96ba-5d02-b426-27fbbc8d3851" 17592186430010 "answer-53183f8e-14d6-52c2-9f1a-70be683a7846" 17592186430011 "answer-5f5eaa2f-7b67-59fb-ac74-96008538d1e1" 17592186430